          "passageTitle": "Cross-Text Connections — Q17",
          "passage": "Text 1:\nIn parts of Yukon, Canada, the lynx is a major\npredator of the arctic ground squirrel.\nResearcher Andrea E. Byrom and colleagues\nfound that when this predation pressure on\narctic ground squirrels was temporarily reduced,\ntheir numbers significantly increased. This\nfinding illustrates a foundational ecological\nprinciple: predators control prey population\nnumbers.\nText 2:\nMark A. Hurley and colleagues found that\nexcluding coyotes from a site in the state of\nIdaho where they typically prey on mule deer\nhad no significant effect on mule deer\nabundance. Many other predation relief studies\nshow an increase in prey abundance, but those\nstudies often focus on small, rapidly reproducing\nprey, like birds, squirrels, and rabbits, rather\nthan large, slowly reproducing prey like mule\ndeer, which could account for the difference\nbetween those results and Hurley and\ncolleagues' results.",
